{
  "scenario": {
    "id": "P02",
    "title": "Boot fault - startup code forgets to zero .bss",
    "layer": "2 Boot",
    "description": "The same program as P01, but the reset handler skips the .bss loop. SRAM is not zero at power-on (the model fills it with seeded pseudo-random words), so the uninitialised global \"counter\" starts with garbage instead of 0 and the program prints a nonsense value. Nothing crashes, which is exactly why this bug is hard to find on real hardware.\n",
    "program": "blink",
    "options": {},
    "faults": [
      {
        "type": "missing_bss_zero"
      }
    ],
    "config": {
      "seed": 1,
      "max_cycles": 50000
    },
    "expected": {
      "summary": "Boot reaches main without faults, but the bss_zeroed step reports garbage and \"counter=\" is not 1.",
      "checks": [
        {
          "check": "boot_step",
          "step": "bss_zeroed",
          "ok": false
        },
        {
          "check": "boot_step",
          "step": "data_copied",
          "ok": true
        },
        {
          "check": "no_faults"
        },
        {
          "check": "uart_contains",
          "text": "boot ok"
        },
        {
          "check": "uart_not_contains",
          "text": "counter=1\n"
        },
        {
          "check": "uart_contains",
          "text": "greeting_len=7"
        }
      ]
    },
    "what_to_look_for": "Compare with P01. The boot_step event for bss_zeroed says the check FAILED and names the global that still holds garbage (counter=0x........). The program then runs normally: \"boot ok\", six LED edges, but the UART line \"counter=\" shows a large random number instead of 1, while \"greeting_len=7\" is still correct because .data was copied. Change the seed in the YAML and the garbage value changes: the symptom is non-deterministic on real hardware.\n",
    "likely_root_causes": [
      "The reset handler has no .bss loop, or loops from __bss_start to __bss_start (wrong end symbol).",
      "The linker script does not define __bss_start/__bss_end, or they are not aligned so the loop stops early.",
      "A .bss section was renamed (.bss.*, COMMON) and the linker script does not collect it into the zeroed range.",
      "Globals placed in .noinit on purpose, then read as if they were zeroed."
    ],
    "how_to_debug": [
      "In GDB after reset, before main: 'print &counter' then 'x/wx &counter'. A non-zero value before any code touched it is the bug.",
      "Run 'arm-none-eabi-nm -n' or 'readelf -S' and check the symbol lives between __bss_start and __bss_end.",
      "Break on main, inspect several .bss globals: if they are all random, the loop never ran; if only the last ones are, the end symbol is wrong.",
      "Power-cycle the board (not just reset): the value changes between power cycles when SRAM retains nothing."
    ],
    "what_this_means": "C promises that globals without an initialiser are zero, but it is the startup code, not the hardware, that keeps that promise. A missing .bss loop produces a program that is correct in a simulator that zeroes memory and wrong on a board whose SRAM wakes up with random contents.\n",
    "hidden_symptom": "LED blinks, 'boot ok' prints, but 'counter=' shows a huge number instead of 1 and it changes between power cycles."
  },
